on to use the Modified Version under the terms of this License, in the form shown in the Addendum below. G. Preserve in that license notice the full lists of Invariant Sections and required Cover Texts given in the Document’s license notice. H. Include an unaltered copy of this License. I.\nL56: Preserve the section entitled \"History\", and its title, and add to it an item stating at least the title, year, new authors, and publisher of the Modified Version as given on the Title Page. If there is no section entitled \"History\" in the Document, create one stating the title, year, authors, and publisher of the Document as given on its Title Page, then add an item describing the Modified Version as stated in the previous sentence. J.\nL57: Preserve the network location, if any, given in the Document for public access to a Transparent copy of the Document, and likewise the network locations given in the Document for previous versions it was based on. These may be placed in the \"History\" section. You may omit a network location for a work that was published at least four years before the Document itself, or if the original publisher of the version it refers to gives permission. K.\nL58: In any section entitled \"Acknowledgements\" or \"Dedications\", preserve the section’s title, and preserve in the section all the substance and tone of each of the contributor acknowledgements and/or dedications given therein. L. Preserve all the Invariant Sections of the Document, unaltered in their text and in their titles. Section numbers or the equivalent are not considered part of the section titles. M. Delete any section entitled \"Endorsements\".\nL59: Such a section may not be included in the Modified Version. N. Do not retitle any existing section as \"Endorsements\" or to conflict in title with any Invariant Section.\nL60: If the Modified Version includes new front-matter sections or appendices that qualify as Secondary Sections and contain no material copied from the Document, you may at your option designate some or all of these sections as invariant. To do this, add their titles to the list of Invariant Sections in the Modified Version’s license notice. These titles must be distinct from any other section titles.\nL61: You may add a section entitled \"Endorsements\", provided it contains nothing but endorsements of your Modified Version by various parties—for example, statements of peer review or that the text has been approved by an organization as the authoritative definition of a standard.\nL62: You may add a passage of up to five words as a Front-Cover Text, and a passage of up to 25 words as a Back-Cover Text, to the end of the list of Cover Texts in the Modified Version. Only one passage of Front-Cover Text and one of Back-Cover Text may be added by (or through arrangements made by) any one entity.\nL63: If the Document already includes a cover text for the same cover, previously added by you or by arrangement made by the same entity you are acting on behalf of, you may not add another; but you may replace the old one, on explicit permission from the previous publisher that added the old one.\nL64: The author(s) and publisher(s) of the Document do not by this License give permission to use their names for publicity for or to assert or imply endorsement of any Modified Version.\nL65: \nL66: 5. COMBINING DOCUMENTS\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nCopyright, Fair Use, and Licensing Statements for SRD, Data, Software, and Technical Series Publications | NIST (https://www.nist.gov/open/copyright-fair-use-and-licensing-statements-srd-data-software-and-technical-series-publications)\nciteturn28view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.nist.gov/open/copyright-fair-use-and-licensing-statements-srd-data-software-and-technical-series-publications\",\"lineno\":175}); Total lines: 230\nL149: You may improve, modify, and create derivative works of the data or any portion of the data, and you may copy and distribute such modifications or works. Modified works should carry a notice stating that you changed the data and should note the date and nature of any such change. Please explicitly acknowledge the National Institute of Standards and Technology as the source of the data:  Data citation recommendations are provided at cite95†https://www.nist.gov/open/license .\nL150: Permission to use this data is contingent upon your acceptance of the terms of this agreement and upon your providing appropriate acknowledgments of NIST’s creation of the data/work.\nL151: ##\nL152: ## Extramural Data\nL153: \nL154:  Describes how NIST may also make available data produced by recipients of a grant, contract or other award if the following criteria are met.\nL155:   1. The awardee is an individual researcher or research group.\nL156:   2. The funding agreement gives NIST the right to make the data public.\nL157:   3. The data has a high potential for re-use, and its continued availability is in NIST’s interest.\nL158:   4. The Federal Program Officer and their Division Chief agree that the data is of sufficient quality for NIST to make it public.\nL159: \nL160: A NIST point of contact must be provided for the entry in NIST’s data inventory, and the following disclaimer must be included:\nL161: This data was produced as part of grant <grant number> with the National Institute of Standards and Technology. NIST provides this data as a public service and assumes no liability whatsoever for use of the data. NIST provides the data “AS IS” and makes NO WARRANTY OF ANY TYPE, EXPRESS OR IMPLIED, INCLUDING WITHOUT LIMITATION NO WARRANTY OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE OR USE, AND NO WARRANTY OF NON-INFRINGEMENT OF THIRD PARTY INTELLECTUAL PROPERTY RIGHTS.\nL162: Users are solely responsible for ensuring the accuracy, currency, and other qualities for use of NIST-provided data or for any products derived from or in connection with the NIST-provided data.\nL163: ## NIST Software\nL164: \nL165: Like non-SRD data, software produced at NIST is generally not subject to copyright protection in the United States, but may be subject to foreign copyright (as per cite104†17 U.S.C. §105†www.govinfo.gov ).  Like data/works, domestic copyright may be held be NIST if copyright is assigned to NIST by a third-party (e.g. contractor).  Distributions of NIST created software, whether in source or compiled form, should include the following licensing statement:\nL166: NIST-developed software is provided by NIST as a public service. You may use, copy, and distribute copies of the software in any medium, provided that you keep intact this entire notice. You may improve, modify, and create derivative works of the software or any portion of the software, and you may copy and distribute such modifications or works. Modified works should carry a notice stating that you changed the software and should note the date and nature of any such change.\nL167: Please explicitly acknowledge the National Institute of Standards and Technology as the source of the software.\nL168: NIST-developed software is expressly provided \"AS IS.\" NIST MAKES NO WARRANTY OF ANY KIND, EXPRESS, IMPLIED, IN FACT, OR ARISING BY OPERATION OF LAW, INCLUDING, WITHOUT LIMITATION, THE IMPLIED WARRANTY OF MERCHANTABILITY, FITNESS FOR A PARTICULAR PURPOSE, NON-INFRINGEMENT, AND DATA ACCURACY. NIST NEITHER REPRESENTS NOR WARRANTS THAT THE OPERATION OF THE SOFTWARE WILL BE UNINTERRUPTED OR ERROR-FREE, OR THAT ANY DEFECTS WILL BE CORRECTED.\nL169: NIST DOES NOT WARRANT OR MAKE ANY REPRESENTATIONS REGARDING THE USE OF THE SOFTWARE OR THE RESULTS THEREOF, INCLUDING BUT NOT LIMITED TO THE CORRECTNESS, ACCURACY, RELIABILITY, OR USEFULNESS OF THE SOFTWARE.\nL170: You are solely responsible for determining the appropriateness of using and distributing the software and you assume all risks associated with its use, including but not limited to the risks and costs of program errors, compliance with applicable laws, damage to or loss of data, programs or equipment, and the unavailability or interruption of operation. This software is not intended to be used in any situation where a failure could cause risk of injury or damage to property.\nL171: The software developed by NIST employees is not subject to copyright protection within the United States.\nL172: Distributions of NIST software should also include copyright and licensing statements of any third-party software that are legally bundled with the code in compliance with the conditions of those licenses.  Contact the NIST Office of Chief Counsel.\nL173: \nL174: Recommendations for citation of software are provided below.\nL175: ## NIST Technical Series Publications\nL176: In accordance with cite105†Public Law 100-519, Section 107 (Oct 24, 1988†www.govinfo.gov ), NIST maintains a research information center to support the research, publishing, and preservation needs required to fulfill the scientific and technical mission of NIST.  This includes publishing the NIST Technical Series: technical reports and Journal of Research of NIST.  Works authored by NIST employees are not subject to Copyright protection within the United States; foreign rights are reserved.  To the extent NIST may assert rights outside of the United States, the public is granted the non-exclusive, perpetual, paid-up, royalty-free, worldwide right to reprint works in all formats including print, electronically, and online, and in all subsequent editions, and derivative works.  Please use the recommended citation format below followed by “Republished courtesy of the National Institute of Standards and Technology.”  Some works published by NIST may have been written by third parties and may be subject to copyright protection.\nL177: ## Citation Recommendations\nL178: Journals often have their own citing formats for publications and other items included in article reference lists. NIST's data and software citation recommendations are consistent in using the same citation elements (author, title, publisher, year of publication, etc.) as used by journals.\nL179: In the absence of other (e.g., journal) defined requirements, these recommendations provide a consistent manner for appropriate attribution to scientific datasets and software that are made available under NIST's public access policy.\nL180: ### Recommended Formats for Data\nL181: \nL182: The following minimum requirements and format should be used to cite NIST datasets (a URL is used if a PID is not available):\nL183: \nL184: #### Recommended Format 1:\nL185: \nL186: Author/editor (Publication Year), Title, Publisher, Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL (Access date)\nL187: #### Example:\nL188: \nL189: Siderius, D.W., Shen, V.K., Johnson III, R.D.,  and van Zee, R.D., eds. (2013), NIST/ARPA-E Database of Novel and Emerging Adsorbent Materials, NIST Standard Reference Database 205, National Institute of Standards and Technology, cite106†https://doi.org/10.18434/T43882†doi.org (Accessed July 13, 2016)\nL190: \nL191: An extended format can be used to include two optional properties, Version and Type of Medium, when appropriate. If so, the recommended format with NIST example is as follows.\nL192: #### Recommended Format 2:\nL193: \nL194: Author/editor (Publication Year), Title, Version, Publisher, [Type of Medium] Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL (Access date)\nL195: \nL196: #### Example:\nL197: \nL198: Sansonetti, J.E., Martin, W.C., and Young, S.L. (2005), NIST Basic Atomic Spectroscopic Data, NIST Standard Reference Database 108, Version 1.1.2, National Institute of Standards and Technology, [Online] cite107†https://doi.org/10.18434/T4FW23†doi.org (Accessed July 13, 2016)\nL199: ### Recommended Format for Software\nL200: \nL201: The use of NIST software can be cited either through a citation to a published literature article describing the software or through a citation of the software itself. Consult the software's home page for the recommended citation.\nL202: If a literature article does not exist, the software citation should use extended format described above, specifying \"[Software]\" as the medium type. If a URL is provided (instead of a PID), it should point to the best \"home page\" for the software product. This may be the root of the software's code repository if a more appropriate home page does not exist. The publication release date should correspond to the release year for the version of the software used.\nL203: #### Example:\nL204: \nL205: Guyer, J.E., Wheeler, D.A., and Warren, J.A. (2013), FiPy: A Finite Volume PDE Solver Using Python, Version 3.1, National Institute of Standards and Technology, [Software], cite108†https://www.ctcms.nist.gov/fipy/†www.ctcms.nist.gov (Accessed May 26, 2015)\nL206: \nL207: ### Recommended Format for NIST Technical Series Publications\nL208: \nL209: Author/editor (Publication Year), Title, Series and Publication Identifier, Publisher, Persistent Identifier (PID) or URL (Access date)\nL210: #### Example:\nL211: \nL212: Joint Task Force Transformation Initiative Interagency Working Group (2013), Security and Privacy Controls for Federal Information Systems and Organizations, NIST Special Publication (SP) 800-53, Rev. 4, Includes updates as of January 22, 2015, National Institute of Standards and Technology, Gaithersburg, MD, cite109†https://doi.org/10.6028/NIST.SP.800-53r4†doi.org (Accessed June 21, 2018)\nL213: \nL214: \nL215: \nL216: cite110†Standards L217: \nL218: Created May 27, 2015, Updated June 24, 2025\nL219: \nL220: cite111†Was this page helpful? L221: ### HEADQUARTERS\nL222: \nL223: 100 Bureau Drive\nL224: Gaithersburg, MD 20899\nL225: 301-975-2000\nL226: \nL227: cite112†Webmaster | cite81†Contact Us | cite113†Our Other Offices L228: \nL229: How are we doing? cite114†Feedback --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nNIST Special Publication XXX (https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2015/NIST.IR.8091.pdf)\nciteturn28view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://nvlpubs.nist.gov/nistpubs/ir/2015/NIST.IR.8091.pdf\",\"lineno\":0}); Total lines: 529\nL0@P0: NISTIR 8091\nL1@P0: Predicting Robot System\nL2@P0: Performance by Measuring and\nL3@P0: Composing Subsystem Attributes\nL4@P0: Michael Shneier\nL5@P0: This publication is available free of charge from:\nL6@P0-1: http://dx.doi.org/10.6028/NIST.IR.8091NISTIR 8091\nL7@P1: Predicting Robot System Performance\nL8@P1: by Measuring and Composing\nL9@P1: Subsystem Attributes\nL10@P1: Michael Shneier\nL11@P1: Intelligent Systems Division\nL12@P1: Engineering Laboratory\nL13@P1: This publication is available free of charge from:\nL14@P1: http://dx.doi.org/10.6028/NIST.IR.8091\nL15@P1: November 2015\nL16@P1: U.S. Department of Commerce\nL17@P1: Penny Pritzker, Secretary\nL18@P1: National Institute of Standards and Technology\nL19@P1-2: Willie May, Under Secretary of Commerce for Standards and Technology and Director i\nL20@P2: Abstract\nL21@P2: A robotic system typically consists of a number of subsystems, for example for sensing,\nL22@P2: manipulation, and motion. Performance measurements are frequently obtained\nL23@P2: independently for each of the subsystems but, because of conflicting interactions, it may\nL24@P2: be difficult to predict the performance of the whole system from these measures. It is\nL25@P2: often useful to separate the independent subsystem measures from those that interact and\nL26@P2: to trade off performance between interacting subsystems to achieve global performance\nL27@P2: goals. This paper discusses the representation and composition of performance measures\nL28@P2: that aim at achieving such a global understanding of a robot system’s performance.\nL29@P2: Keywords: Manufacturing System; Performance Evaluation; Performance Measures;\nL30@P2: Robotics; Taxonomy.\nL31@P2: Disclaimer: Commercial equipment and materials are identified in order to adequately\nL32@P2: specify certain procedures. In no case does such identification imply recommendation or\nL33@P2: endorsement by the National Institute of Standards and Technology, nor does it imply\nL34@P2: that the materials or equipment identified are necessarily the best available for the\nL35@P2-3: purpose.1\nL36@P3: 1. Introduction\nL37@P3: Robotic workcells are complex systems made up of components that may themselves be\nL38@P3: complex. Setting up a workcell typically requires the expertise of a robot integrator who\nL39@P3: selects the components and designs their interactions to achieve the desired functionality\nL40@P3: and throughput. This process requires a lot of experience and there are few guidelines for\nL41@P3: how to select components. Usually there are many competing criteria that must be taken\nL42@P3: into consideration, including cost, throughput, reliability, ease of setup and use, and\nL43@P3: quality of the products produced. Ideally, the system would be made up of subsystems,\nL44@P3: each of which is the best available within the budget, resulting in a total system that is,\nL45@P3: similarly, as good as can be obtained for the price. Unfortunately, the definition of “best”\nL46@P3: for a subsystem is usually dependent on the role the subsystem plays in the overall\nL47@P3: workcell and there are often conflicts between the requirements of the subsystems that\nL48@P3: may make it impossible to use them in the same system. As a result, it is very difficult to\nL49@P3: develop a principled approach to workcell design and most implementations involve\nL50@P3: tradeoffs simply to get the system to work.\nL51@P3: While many design problems are indeed intractable, it would be very useful to know\nL52@P3: which ones are not and to understand what makes a given problem unsolvable. This\nL53@P3: would enable a more structured approach to workcell design and let tradeoffs be made\nL54@P3: with greater understanding of what the real issues are. Given that high-level system\nL55@P3: performance goals are frequently much less constrained than lower-level goals, this might\nL56@P3: make it easier to achieve an approximation of the ideal solution. This paper proposes an\nL57@P3: approach to design that starts with performance measures for the subsystems and overall\nL58@P3: system performance goals. In principle, it should be possible to decompose the problem\nL59@P3: into one of three classes, from simplest to most difficult:\nL60@P3: – There is no interaction between the subsystems or any interactions do not place\nL61@P3: constraints on the impacted subsystems.\nL62@P3: – The subsystems interact but the constraints induced by the interactions are\nL63@P3: satisfiable.\nL64@P3: – The subsystems interact and the constraints are not satisfiable (note that\nL65@P3: establishing this may not be decidable).\nL66@P3: In the first case, it is easy to build a system by selecting subsystems that meet the overall\nL67@P3: goals such as performance, cost, and reliability. In the second case, it is more difficult\nL68@P3: because selecting a subsystem might place constraints on how the other subsystems may\nL69@P3: be chosen or may bound the parameters required for another subsystem (e.g., by requiring\nL70@P3: a maximum time to complete a step of the task or a minimum quality of fit to prevent\nL71@P3: later jamming). This leads to a combinatorial problem, but one that can be solved either\nL72@P3: analytically or numerically. The last case is not solvable but it may be possible to\nL73@P3: determine what is preventing a solution and develop an approximation, either by\nL74@P3: modifying the overall system goals or by limiting the impact to a defined class of parts\nL75@P3: (e.g., if the system was designed to make a range of parts, perhaps a subset of the range\nL76@P3-4: can still be produced). 2\nL77@P4: The need for establishing global performance measures through composition of local\nL78@P4: measures is not restricted to manufacturing robotics. For example, a recent report from\nL79@P4: the United States Department of Defense Research and Engineering Community of\nL80@P4: Interest on Automation [1] describes the need in the test and evaluation of autonomous\nL81@P4: systems for composable measures of performance, although the report does not suggest\nL82@P4: any specific measures.\nL83@P4: 2. Related Work\nL84@P4: In general, the performance of a robotic system as a whole is measured with respect to\nL85@P4: the task being accomplished or the goals of the company making the products, while the\nL86@P4: performance of each subsystem is measured against more limited and specific criteria.\nL87@P4: There may not be a direct relationship between the overall goals and the individual\nL88@P4: subsystem goals, but a path must be established that links them if it is to be possible to\nL89@P4: predict the overall performance from that of the components. While this is frequently an\nL90@P4: ad hoc process, a number of multi-objective optimization techniques have been applied to\nL91@P4: try to bridge the gap. Other approaches have included using simulations or methods based\nL92@P4: on fuzzy reasoning or genetic algorithms. Much of the literature is focused on the\nL93@P4: problem of job shop scheduling, which involves assigning tasks to a set of machines so as\nL94@P4: to optimize production. While this is not exactly the same problem discussed here, the\nL95@P4: solution approaches are applicable.\nL96@P4: Several researchers have developed simulation-based systems for manufacturing\nL97@P4: planning. In a European Esprit project, Valckenaers, et al. [2] developed a benchmarking\nL98@P4: system that made use of a web-based simulation. It consists of three parts: a testbench\nL99@P4: assistant that interactively helps set up the problem, the emulator that actually carries out\nL100@P4: the simulation and collects statistics, and the performance evaluation component that\nL101@P4: computes a range of measures that can be used to evaluate how well different control\nL102@P4: schemes work. Xing, et al. [3] define a simulation model to solve the multi-objective\nL103@P4: flexible job shop scheduling problem. They developed their simulation using Matlab and\nL104@P4: developed a model consisting of six components: the input component that defines the\nL105@P4: problem and its associated data; the operation assignment component that optimizes the\nL106@P4: assignment of operations to machines; the operation sequencing component that provides\nL107@P4: the flow of operations through machines; the objective evaluation component that creates\nL108@P4: a schedule of operations; the control component that is responsible for flow control and\nL109@P4: ensures that the scheduled operation sequence is feasible; and the output component that\nL110@P4: presents the optimization results to the user. In a different application, that of\nL111@P4: Input/output subsystems in a computer operating system, Ganger and Patt [4] show how a\nL112@P4: simulation enables understanding of subtle issues that arise when performance measures\nL113@P4: of subsystems are rolled up into performance measures for a whole system.\nL114@P4: A system that is designed with goals very much in line with what is proposed here is Shin\nL115@P4: and Park [5]. Their goal is to address engineering problems in which multiple subsystems\nL116@P4: are involved, each of which uses different performance measures. The relationships\nL117@P4: between subsystems are such that inputs for one subsystem are outputs from another. In\nL118@P4: their approach, the system is decomposed into subsystems corresponding to the parts for\nL119@P4-5: which performance values have been defined. An optimization is carried out for each 3\nL120@P5: subsystem using its own design variables, objective function, and constraints. In these\nL121@P5: optimizations, the coupling terms with other subsystems are treated as constants. The\nL122@P5: coupled physics is solved separately and transferred to the individual subsystems and the\nL123@P5: process is iterated until the convergence criteria are satisfied.\nL124@P5: Genetic algorithms are another commonly-used approach to the challenges of combining\nL125@P5: multiple components into a working system that addresses the constraints imposed by\nL126@P5: subsystems. Sivakumar, et al. [6] apply a genetic approach to the problem of tolerance\nL127@P5: allocation across a number of manufacturing subsystems. Here, the goal is to maintain the\nL128@P5: tolerance requirements of parts as they are processed during manufacture. This affects the\nL129@P5: choice of machines to use and processing methods to apply. They use two different\nL130@P5: genetic algorithms to search for optimal solutions and show numerically the success of\nL131@P5: their proposed methods.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn28view3 [wordlim: 200] Unable to resolve screenshot call because content type is not application/pdf and web screenshot is not enabled\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn28view4 [wordlim: 200] Unable to resolve screenshot call because content type is not application/pdf and web screenshot is not enabled\n"
